  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#beyond"/>
    <link type="seealso" xref="programs"/>
    <title type="sort">5</title>
    <desc>The messages, a brick that doesn't start, a refused start, a project that can't be read.</desc>
  </info>
  <title>When something goes wrong</title>
  <section id="logs">
    <title>The messages</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Logs</gui></guiseq> has the messages of the run: those of Virtualbricks, of its bricks, and what their programs print. <code>level:warning</code> in its filter keeps the warnings and the errors, with what the programs wrote on their standard error; <code>source:tap1</code> keeps the messages of the brick <sys>tap1</sys>. All about the window is in <link xref="logs"/>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="start">
    <title>A brick doesn't start</title>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p><gui>Not connected</gui>: a plug of the brick goes nowhere. Use <gui>Connect To</gui> in its menu, or its settings.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p><gui>Not configured</gui>: a setting it needs is missing; its settings say which.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p><gui>Error</gui>, on the switch wrapper it plugs into: the switch of that folder isn't there; the tooltip of the state of the switch wrapper says why. Start the switch again, or give the switch wrapper the folder of a switch that runs.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>A warning sign in <gui>New Brick</gui>, or a message about a program: the program isn't installed; see <link xref="programs"/>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>Otherwise, filter the <gui>Logs</gui> window with <code>source:</code> and the name of the brick, as <code>source:tap1</code>: its message <sys>Starting:</sys> has the command it runs, and the program says why it stopped.</p>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
  <section id="sudo">
    <title>A tap or a capture interface doesn't start</title>
    <p>They run as root, through <cmd>sudo</cmd>, which can't ask for your password on a terminal: Virtualbricks runs it without one. So either:</p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>a helper asks for the password in a window: install one, as <sys>ssh-askpass</sys>, and name it in the variable <sys>SUDO_ASKPASS</sys> or in a line <code>Path askpass /usr/bin/ssh-askpass</code> of <file>/etc/sudo.conf</file>; or</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <p>a rule of <file>sudoers</file> lets you run <cmd>vde_plug2tap</cmd> and <cmd>vde_pcapplug</cmd> without a password, with <sys>NOPASSWD</sys>, as a computer without a display needs.</p>
      </item>
    </list>
    <p>Otherwise <cmd>sudo</cmd> refuses at once, and the <gui>Logs</gui> window shows what it said.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="lock">
    <title>Virtualbricks doesn't start</title>
    <p>Only one Virtualbricks runs on the computer, unless it is started otherwise; a second one is refused, and the message says which process holds the lock, and whose it is. To run a lab beside another, start each in its own workspace, with <cmd>virtualbricks --workspace <var>folder</var></cmd>; see <link xref="console"/>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="project">
    <title>A project can't be read</title>
    <p>If the project that was open last can't be opened at start, the Projects window opens and says why. A project written by a newer Virtualbricks is not read, and not written over. Another problem in the file, as a value out of range, takes the default and is reported in the <gui>Logs</gui> window, with the setting it is about.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="report">
    <title>Reporting a problem</title>
    <p>Tell us what broke at <link href="https://github.com/virtualsquare/virtualbricks/issues">github.com/virtualsquare/virtualbricks/issues</link>, and attach the messages: <gui>Save…</gui>, in the menu of the <gui>Logs</gui> window, writes them to a file; see <link xref="logs#save"/>.</p>
  </section>
</page>
